<p>CPI-W--Index for urban wage earners and clerical workers.</p>
<p>Wall Street economists expected a 0.1 percent rise in April consumer prices. Excluding food and energy, they expected a 0.2 percent increase.  </p>
<p>Fresh vegetable prices in April fell 9.1 percent, the largest decline since June 1993 when prices were 10.6 percent lower, Labor Department said. Tomato prices fell 13.8 percent, the largest decline since a 16.4 percent fall in June 1996.</p>
<p>Apparel and upkeep prices rose 0.9 percent, the steepest rise since 1.1 percent in February 1993. Tobacco and smoking product prices rose 2.4 percent, the largest increase since 2.5 percent in July 1990.</p>
